azgor wrote:
> 1: The ability to turn bags of holding inside out. Everything in the
> dungeon gets heavier or lighter, depending on b/u/c, and if there are
> any bags of holding, tricks, or /oCancel in the dungeon, it explodes. -
> speedevil

Dear god, why?

> 2: Dirt - Your charisma degrades gradually as you dungeon crawl due to
> the accumulation of dirt. Swimming or the application of a towel will
> restore your natural charisma for a while.  One would become dirtier
> faster the more one fought. - azgor

 Charisma isn't just physical beauty, but likability and presence too.
Anyway, for it to maintain parity with the other abilities unicorn
horns would have to cure this charisma loss, and it doesn't makes quite
so much sense for unicorns to *clean* you.

> 3: Cannibalism penalties should only apply once per corpse. - ekiM

I take it you mean that if you eat a same-race corpse once, but are
interrupted, they happen again when you resume?  Hmm, if that's the
case... I don't know, it depends on the intended nature of the penalty.
 This YANI may well be just a suggested bug fix.  Try submitting it to
the devteam.

> 4: Shuriken shouldn't disappear. They're solid metal, like daggers,
> they shouldn't disappear. And then they'd be a somewhat viable weapon
> without being enchanted up.

Don't daggers disappear sometimes when thrown?  Why should shuriken be
any different?  Actually, since shuriken are the strongest of the
thrown weapons, an argument could be made that they should use up
*faster* than other types.  Those little sharp edges dull easily....

> 5: for monks, enchantment to worn gloves should act the same as an
> enchanment to a held weapon for other roles [maybe applies to all
> roles, but ony for weaponless combat? -ed]

Interesting.  This could require a bit of rebalancing for the Monk
class but it may be reasonable.  Worth thinking about.

> 6: Maybe breaking "role conducts" could have a more serious affect than
> just -1 alignment. Maybe -10% of your current maxalignment? Or some hit
> to luck?

I agree that alignment penalties are pretty laughable.  Luck hits are
potentially very great penalties (because they could easily make it
unsafe to pray), so I don't know if that's a good idea.

> 7: Monkeys need to be tameable with bananas.

I do like the idea, but I'd guess if the devteam were going to
implement this they would have done it when they added monkeys to the
game.

> 8: Crushing a monster carrying a wand with a drawbridge should explode
> the wand. - Grunt

What happens to the wand in the current game?  Does a monster's
inventory get destroyed when it gets crushed, or just dumped into the
drink?  If destroyed, then I agree that the wand thing is only logical,
and also say it would have good balance, due to it providing a
potential drawback to the tactic of standing back and playing the
passtune over and over to empty out the courtyard.  But if crushed
monsters' inventories *are* destroyed, what happens if that monster is
carrying an indestructible item, like the sanctum items or the Amulet?

> 9: flying (as a monster) should be voluntarily controlled, perhaps by <
> and > or by #fly. While flying, nutrition should be consumed at a
> faster rate. - azgor

Interesting.  To my knowledge, only Vampire Lords can currently switch
at will.  I'd say that most floating monsters (lights, eyes, spheres)
should not be able to land at will, but an argument could be made for
other types.  Increased food consumption also makes sense, both
logically and concerning balance, although since polymorphing resets
your hunger counter it wouldn't often come into play (and wouldn't
matter at all if the monster doesn't need to eat).

> 10: Fake amulets of yendor should asplode your container, so you can't
> trivially figure them out by that
> method. Or is that too evil? - Eidolos

Way too evil.  Think of it from the perspective of a newbie player.  He
doesn't yet know that the Big Stuff can't be confined by those
trappings.  He might honestly attempt to conceal the Amulet, and get
his first really great game almost ruined basically due to programmer
malice.  But this isn't even necessary: there is a patch in existence
that allows the big items to be stashed yet isn't (too) unbalanced.

> 11: If you hit a monster with a ?oFire, it should burn the monster and
> be used up.  Similarly, hitting it with a ?oConfuse Monster or a
> ?oScare Monster will confuse or scare it. - Grunt

I am *all* for extra non-traditional uses for items.  This is cool,
although except for the fire thing not really too useful.

> 12: Scroll of Jotting: Once acquired, it allows the player to write
> notes and reminders during the game for later viewing.  This would
> eliminate the need to name items with the desired information. -
> aristotle73

How about just making it so that magic markers could be used in
conjunction with blank paper to produce notes?  That could be open up
to some of the same kinds of abuses that Scrolls of Mail would be if it
weren't for some special cases in the code that disallow it.

> 13: The demon summoned from sacrificing should actually serve some
> purpose instead of being merely an annoyance.  The demon should give
> the player a random (but useful) item or an adequate sum of gold before
> becoming a peaceful nuisance.

I do like this idea, but it'd have to be handled carefully, lest the
player profit from it too much.  (Note that non-chaotics end up with a
hostile demon lord instead of a peaceful one.)

> 14: #dragging corpses that are too heavy to lift.  (Use some of the
> iron ball mechanics for this, perhaps.)  Attempting to #drag a dragon
> should produce YAFM: "He's already a-draggin'".

I like the idea, it's annoying when the corpse is right next to the
altar but can't be sacrificed because it can't be carried.  Of course,
nothing's stopping you from just dropping enough stuff to be able to
lift it.

> 15: Monsters might use wands of digging to dig paths within a level, as
> well as just digging down ASAP.

Interesting, but potentially difficult to implement, since if done
badly monsters will waste wand charges making useless passages and then
be unable to use their wand for escaping.  Anyway, I'd suggest that
minotaurs (frequent bearers of wands of digging) be disallowed from
using wands for this purpose, since they'd be reluctant to damage their
mazes.

> 16: New engulfing attack: suffocation (behaves similarly to
> "oStrangulation).  Gelatinous cubes are a good
>  candidate for this, as it's their canonical behaviour.  Perhaps water
> elementals and some of the oozes/puddings too. (Patch in progress.) -
> Grunt

It would increase the utility of wands of digging, but those are
already among the most useful wands.  It would make those monsters much
more dangerous however.  I think, were this implemented, that there
should also be added a special way to get out of them that's accessible
to most players besides prayer (which I think will get you unengulfed
anyway).

> 17: If too many gremlins are spawned, whether tame, peaceful or
> otherwise, a small furry creature should appear and annihilate some of
> the gremlins (see SGrunt's 7/29/06 game for the reason).

Can't you just tell us?  Is this supposed to be a reference to the
movie Gremlins?  I think gremlin overpopulation is a potential hassle,
I'm not sure such a jokey answer is the way to handle it.

> 18: Silver-trimmed gauntlets. Identical to leather gloves but your
> unarmed attacks now do silver damage.

Interesting.  Would help out Monks.  If the player doesn't happen upon
a silver weapon then this could make an interesting alternative,
especially since it would give players a reason to go unarmed in some
circumstances.

- John H.



